1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,144
If an AI writes the code today, who pays for
it when somebody has to change it next year?

2
00:00:06,667 --> 00:00:15,259
One research team stopped guessing and ran a randomised
trial: code written with or without AI, then changed by hand.

3
00:00:15,267 --> 00:00:27,507
The worry has a familiar shape: one study collected in a review of the research warns that code built
up through conversation often "lacks cohesive structure", which makes later maintenance harder.

4
00:00:27,533 --> 00:00:39,581
And on security, the Razorwire Cybersecurity channel put it bluntly: "46% of
AI generated code is reportedly uh contain some form of security weakness".

5
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:45,140
So what happened in that trial when people
had to change code an AI had helped write?

6
00:00:45,167 --> 00:00:50,999
On speed, nothing reliable: their completion
times were consistent with no effect at all.

7
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:59,952
The authors are careful about what that means: any difference was at most
small, and highly uncertain, which is not the same as proof there is none.

8
00:01:00,467 --> 00:01:08,795
On quality, the code's health score after the changes showed no reliable
difference either, and test coverage was essentially identical.

9
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:18,592
A second, Bayesian analysis even hinted at a small gain in code health
when the original was written with AI by someone who uses it habitually.

10
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:25,968
Dave Farley, talking about the study, summed it up: "The AI
didn't make it any better and it didn't make it any worse."

11
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,256
So where does the debt
fear get its evidence?

12
00:01:29,267 --> 00:01:41,099
Partly from surveys: one study measured maintainability and technical debt through its respondents'
answers to questionnaire scales, and AI use went with lower maintainability and more debt.

13
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:47,040
That is an association in people's answers, not
a timed test of someone else changing the code.

14
00:01:47,067 --> 00:01:55,419
The review of the research counted the worry too: maintainability
concerns came up in 11 of its sources, 23 percent.

15
00:01:55,733 --> 00:02:00,725
That counts how often the concern is raised,
which is not the same as measuring it.

16
00:02:00,733 --> 00:02:10,765
Then there is code out in the wild: a study that tracked commits made with AI coding
assistants found that some of the issues they introduced still survive in the latest code.

17
00:02:10,767 --> 00:02:22,455
The assistants fixed about as many code smells, surface problems like naming and formatting,
as they introduced; but they introduced more correctness and security issues than they fixed.

18
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:32,283
But the authors did not compare those commits with code written by people
alone, so the study cannot say AI code leaves more debt than human code does.

19
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:44,800
And the security worry? One study did put a number on it: of 733 code
snippets generated by AI, 27.3 percent contained security weaknesses.

20
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:58,768
A newer audit went further: researchers sampled deployed web apps built at least 90 percent by AI
agents, confirmed each finding by hand, and found at least one vulnerability in over 90 percent of them.

21
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:07,512
Both are counts of weaknesses in AI code alone; neither can tell you
whether code written by hand would score any better on the same check.

22
00:03:07,833 --> 00:03:26,625
One study that did compare, in open-source repositories, found code written by AI agents introduced security findings at 1.14 times the
rate of human code, line for line, and the most severe ones at 1.51 times, all flagged by an automated scanner rather than confirmed.

23
00:03:26,633 --> 00:03:35,945
The review of the research found security risk was the challenge its sources
raised most, in 10 of them, half of those it tallied for that question.

24
00:03:35,967 --> 00:03:45,423
What those sources agree on is narrower than a rate: generated code may
contain weaknesses when developers rely on it without rigorous checking.

25
00:03:45,933 --> 00:03:51,429
There is one cost, though, that experiments did
measure, and it lands on the person who wrote the code.

26
00:03:51,933 --> 00:04:04,341
In one trial, crowd workers recruited online, most of them freelance or professional software developers,
did a coding task with or without AI, then took a quiz on the skills the task should have built.

27
00:04:04,367 --> 00:04:12,623
Using AI did not make them significantly faster, and their
scores came out lower by a gap worth 17 percent of the quiz.

28
00:04:12,633 --> 00:04:23,769
In a separate experiment with different participants, working with AI cut their chance
of answering questions about their own code correctly by 5.95 percentage points.

29
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:31,732
The authors re-checked it other ways: each time the drop kept
its direction, though in one it was too weak to be sure of.

30
00:04:32,733 --> 00:04:46,677
So, is AI-written code harder to maintain? In the one trial that tested it, over the time it ran, no: no reliable
difference in speed or code health, though one trial with that much uncertainty cannot rule out a small one.

31
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:55,264
The louder maintenance warnings rest on questionnaires, counts of
concern and issue tallies with nothing human to compare them to.

32
00:04:55,267 --> 00:05:07,435
Security is where the worry holds up: most of the AI-built apps that audit sampled had at least one
vulnerability, and against human code the rate was higher, more so for the most severe findings.

33
00:05:08,467 --> 00:05:22,963
The cost that was measured is different: in both of those experiments, people working with AI learned less or understood their own code
less well, and whether that makes it harder for those same people to change their code later is something none of these studies tested.

34
00:05:23,967 --> 00:05:32,655
Simon Willison draws his line here: "I don't think you should ever commit a
line of code that a language model wrote If you don't understand it yourself".

35
00:05:32,667 --> 00:05:41,907
So when you review AI-assisted code, yours or a colleague's, don't stop at
whether it works: ask the author to walk you through it with the AI closed.

36
00:05:42,233 --> 00:05:45,329
If they can't, the code
isn't finished yet.
